d_str = f\"{total_duration:.1f}s\" if current_state == self.STATE_RESPONSE_COMPLETED else \"INCOMPLETE\"\n968: \n969:         t_req_total = time.time() - t_req_start\n970:         self.last_timings = {\n971:             \"pre_exec\": t_pre_exec,\n972:             \"generation\": total_duration,\n973:             \"total\": t_req_total,\n974:             \"turn_detected\": t_turn_detected - t_submit if t_turn_detected else None,\n975:             \"first_token\": t_first_token - t_submit if t_first_token else None,\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":11017,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T16:59:33Z","thinking":"Initial hypothesis: the model is either still in a thinking phase (no output yet), or has prematurely stopped execution. Checking if a timeout condition triggered if the first token is taking too long to generate. If the model has started generating text, but is not running, investigate the execution state. Otherwise, examine the logic associated with a running model.\n\n\nThe timeouts were misconfigured, prematurely terminating requests during prolonged thinking phases or periods of text stagnation. A \"trigger-based architecture\" seems more appropriate; specifically, the model's active state becomes the sole determinant of whether to enforce timeouts, preventing premature termination while the generation is active. A brief stability timer should then begin only *after* the model signals its completion, by transitioning to a non-active state.\n\n\nA more robust state management approach is needed, specifically for cases of empty responses or failure to start. The logic must account for scenarios where the process concludes without producing any output or if the generation doesn't even begin, setting appropriate timeouts. Examining the code, I see the necessary adjustments are in `app.py`, focusing on `get_adaptive_timeouts` and `js_fetch_state`, to implement the trigger-based behavior.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"D:/AI_Engineer/MSEMAX-GAIStudio-vision/app.py","EndLine":696,"StartLine":645,"toolAction":"Viewing run button wait in app.py","toolSummary":"View run button wait in app.py"}}]}
