{"step_index":11019,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T16:59:48Z","thinking":"Initial assessment suggests that handling large image uploads requires extending the timeout duration. The current 50-iteration loop, designed to account for image upload delays, might be insufficient; a safer adjustment to 60 or 80 iterations is warranted. The goal is to investigate and modify the timeout handling mechanism within the application to accommodate potentially long upload times, particularly for high-resolution images.\n\n\nThe timeout configuration leverages UI triggers to determine the generation state, differentiating between active, completed, and idle modes based on button states. This trigger-based approach aims to prevent premature timeouts during active generation. The timeout configuration includes a startup grace period, a stability wait time, and a hard ceiling for safety.\n\n\nExamining UI elements for a \"cancel\" action, looking at button labels (aria-label, tooltip text, inner text) and icons. Also, reviewing DOM tooltips for relevant phrases like \"cancel generation\". This helps to identify any UI controls signaling an active process or generation. The goal is to detect and respond to generation state signals.\n\n\nEvaluating the presence of \"cancel\" indicators: checking for dedicated cancel buttons, DOM tooltips with phrases like \"cancel generation,\" and Angular Material progress spinners or thinking activity markers.  Inspecting the run control button attributes, including `aria-disabled`, `aria-label`, and tooltip text, for \"cancel\" or \"stop\" phrases to determine its state.  If a cancel indicator exists or the run button has cancel/stop wording, the generation is marked as cancellable. Otherwise, the run button state is evaluated (disabled or active).\n\n\nAnalyzing a UI interaction model to determine if a generation process is active or cancellable. The model focuses on detecting specific UI element states and attributes to trigger generation-related actions. The analysis incorporates error handling to manage potential JavaScript evaluation failures, with a retry mechanism and an abort condition for persistent errors.\n\n\nMonitoring the generation state, examining button states, error flags, and text length to determine active generation. Logging state transitions triggered by button changes for debugging. If an error occurs during an inactive state, an error message is yielded. Transitions from the \"no response started\" to \"response in progress\" state if generation is active.\n\n\nGeneration start time is being tracked, and the transition to the \"response in progress\" state is logged, along with trigger details and initial text length. Failure to start generation within the configured timeout results in an error state. Detecting the first character indicates the generation has started. The growth of generated text is monitored to generate incremental updates. Resetting the stability timer to zero if new text appears.\n\n\nChecking if the generation is still active, indicated by a \"cancel\" button state or spinner activity; if inactive, a stability timer begins. If the stability duration elapses without new text, the state is marked as \"completed.\" The current state is always yielded. If the process reaches a hard ceiling without completion, an error is logged.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"D:/AI_Engineer/MSEMAX-GAIStudio-vision/app.py","EndLine":490,"StartLine":442,"toolAction":"Viewing get_adaptive_timeouts lines in app.py","toolSummary":"View get_adaptive_timeouts lines"}}]}
